- op: create  # a93b
  at: '2026-04-16T19:07:56Z'  # a93b
  by: agent  # a93b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a93b
  clock: 1  # a93b
  nonce: a93b  # a93b
  data:  # a93b
    kind: invariant  # a93b
    title: "INV: HTTP-linker URL extraction folds constant-composed URLs across all source languages"  # a93b
    status: needs_human_review  # a93b
    priority: 4  # a93b
    parent:  # a93b
    tags:  # a93b
    - cross_language_linkers  # a93b
    - analysis_quality  # a93b
    isbefore: []  # a93b
    duplicate_of: []  # a93b
    not_duplicate_of: []  # a93b
    pr_ref:  # a93b
    description: "Umbrella invariant covering per-language URL-folding gaps in the HTTP linker. Instances:\n  - WI-rosan: Elm let-bound + String.join URL idioms\n  - WI-sijoh: JS/TS backtick template literals with module-constant interpolation\n\nFuture instances (not filed; no signal yet): Python f-strings, Go fmt.Sprintf, Kotlin/Ruby/Scala interpolation, Java String.format / StringBuilder.\n\nFiled at P4 / needs_human_review because there is no acute signal today beyond the Alertmanager and Prometheus mantine-ui gaps already covered by the two instance items. Purpose is to capture the generalised thinking so that when bakeoff or UAT surfaces analogous gaps in other languages we attach sibling items under this umbrella rather than re-deriving the abstraction.\n\nClosure criterion: either (a) every language whose route-detector is active also has a URL-folding extractor that passes a corpus-wide 'composed URL recovery' test, OR (b) a deliberate scope decision is recorded documenting which languages are intentionally left with literal-only extraction and why."  # a93b
    fields:  # a93b
      statement: "The cross-language HTTP linker's URL extraction stage recovers the literal skeleton of a composed URL — regardless of how that URL is built in the source language — and passes that skeleton to the route-matcher. Variable segments may remain as holes (matched against parameterised routes); literal segments must be folded through constants, let-bindings, template literals, f-strings, sprintf-class formatters, and string-composition expressions so that route-identity fragments are visible."  # a93b
      root_cause: "Every source language composes URLs via a different syntactic mechanism (JS/TS template literals, Python f-strings, Go fmt.Sprintf, Kotlin/Ruby/Scala interpolation, Elm ++ and String.join, Java String.format / StringBuilder, etc.). The current scanners extract URLs only when they appear as a bare string literal or bare identifier at the call site. Any language whose idiomatic URL-composition form differs from that is silently invisible to the HTTP linker even though the route matcher itself could handle the folded output."  # a93b
- op: discuss  # a75c
  at: '2026-05-12T20:39:48Z'  # a75c
  by: agent  # a75c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a75c
  clock: 2  # a75c
  nonce: a75c  # a75c
  message: "Tier 2 verification: both currently-filed instance items (WI-rosan Elm let-bound + String.join, WI-sijoh JS/TS template literals) are shipped and bakeoff-validated. However, the invariant's closure criterion ('every language whose route-detector is active also has a URL-folding extractor that passes a corpus-wide composed-URL recovery test') is NOT met — this item's description explicitly anticipates Python f-strings, Go fmt.Sprintf, Kotlin/Ruby/Scala interpolation, and Java String.format/StringBuilder as 'future instances (not filed; no signal yet)'. Status correctly remains needs_human_review until either (a) sibling items are filed for those languages and resolved, or (b) a deliberate scope decision is recorded documenting which languages are intentionally left at literal-only extraction."  # a75c
- op: discuss  # e4b3
  at: '2026-05-17T09:03:33Z'  # e4b3
  by: agent  # e4b3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e4b3
  clock: 3  # e4b3
  nonce: e4b3  # e4b3
  message: "Cross-item review 2026-05-17: human direction received — pursue option (a) of the closure criterion (every active route-detector language gets URL folding) via a per-idiom YAML / hybrid-engine design. Refined closure criterion below replaces the original (a/b) phrasing; original remains in the description as authored.\n\nREFINED CLOSURE CRITERION: Either (a) every language whose route detector is active has a YAML entry under packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/url_folding/ for at least one composition idiom, OR (b) that language is listed in url_folding/SCOPE.md as intentionally literal-only with a one-line justification. Enforced by a property test parametrized over active_route_languages — fails if neither condition holds.\n\nDESIGN OVERVIEW. Per-idiom YAML files (not per-language) because the math is ~5 idioms × ~10 languages, and idioms are the durable structure. Hybrid engine — YAML declares language-specific call-site regexes and points at a Python engine; existing _fold_template_literal and _fold_elm_string_join generalize and stay as engines. Phased migration as four child WIs filed under this umbrella with isbefore chain A→B→C→D:\n\n- Phase A (refactor, no new languages): extract engines, YAML the existing JS/TS template-literal and Elm String.join.\n- Phase B (string_interpolation expansion): Python f-strings, Kotlin, Ruby, Scala, Swift.\n- Phase C (printf_format idiom): Go fmt.Sprintf, Java String.format, Python %/.format.\n- Phase D (string_concat idiom): JS, Java, Python, Go, Ruby operator concatenation.\n\nINV closes as satisfied once Phase A ships the property test AND a non-trivial subset of phases B-D ship OR an explicit SCOPE.md entry exists for each uncovered active route-language."  # e4b3
